{
  "result": "The first time I truly understood the ocean was not at the beach, but in a library. I was nine, sitting cross-legged on carpet that smelled faintly of old paper, holding an atlas that was bigger than my lap. The blue took up most of the page. Someone had drawn thin, curved lines to show depth, and tiny illustrations of whales and ships that looked impossibly small against all that water. I remember tracing the Mariana Trench with my finger and feeling vertigo, even though I was safely indoors. \n\nThat feeling has never left me: that knowledge can make you dizzy. We collect facts to feel secure, to map the world, but every true fact opens a door to a larger mystery. You learn that octopuses have three hearts and suddenly you have to wonder what it feels like to have three. You learn that light from some stars has traveled for billions of years and you realize you are looking into ancient time. \n\nMaybe that is why I love small, ordinary rituals. Making coffee in the morning is not just caffeine; it is an anchor. The kettle whistles, the steam rises, the day begins. In a universe so vast and strange, we make meaning in repetitions. We choose to care for a plant, to write a sentence, to remember someone's name. None of these will change the tides, but they change us, which might be enough. Enough to keep tracing maps, making coffee, and wondering what comes next together.",
